CAT = "iespAccidents"; SEG = "ACC"
# Unit 3 of Safe Pass: site accident reporting. Safety, Health and Welfare at Work (Reporting of
# Accidents and Dangerous Occurrences) Regulations 2016 as summarised by the HSA; General Application
# Regulations 2007 Part 7 (first aid); emergency numbers 112 and 999.
Q = [
('A worker injured on site cannot do their normal work for more than how many days before the HSA must be told?',
 'Three consecutive days', ['One working day', 'Seven consecutive days', 'Ten working days in a row'],
 'Under the 2016 reporting regulations, an injury must be reported if the employee cannot carry out normal work for more than three consecutive days, not counting the day of the accident.'),
('When counting days off for an HSA accident report, how is the day of the accident treated?',
 'It is not counted', ['It counts as a full day', 'It counts as half a day', 'It counts only if after noon'],
 'The HSA says the more-than-three-consecutive-days test excludes the day of the accident itself.'),
('A worker is killed in a fall on site. How quickly must the HSA or the Gardaí be told?',
 'Immediately', ['Within 24 hours', 'Within five working days', 'At the next safety meeting'],
 'Fatal accidents must be reported immediately to the HSA or the Gardaí, with the formal report to the HSA within five working days of the death.'),
('Who has the legal duty to report a reportable injury of an employee to the HSA?',
 'The employer', ['The injured worker', "The worker's GP", "The site's neighbours"],
 'The 2016 regulations place the duty to report on employers and the self-employed. Workers should still tell their supervisor at once so the report can be made.'),
('Within what time should a non-fatal reportable accident be reported to the HSA?',
 'Ten working days', ['One working day', 'Five working days', 'Thirty working days'],
 'HSA guidance says non-fatal accidents and dangerous occurrences should be reported within ten working days of the event.'),
('A crane overturns on site but nobody is hurt. How is this classed for reporting?',
 'A dangerous occurrence', ['A minor incident only', 'A near miss with no report', 'A design fault for the PSDP'],
 'Schedule 15 of the General Application Regulations, added by the 2016 reporting regulations, lists the collapse or overturning of lifting equipment, or failure of a load-bearing part, as a dangerous occurrence, reportable to the HSA even when nobody is injured.'),
('A worker trips over a cable but is not hurt. What should they do?',
 'Report the near miss to the supervisor', ['Say nothing, as nobody was hurt', "Report it straight to the HSA's online system", 'Move the cable and forget about it'],
 'Near misses show where the next injury will come from. Reporting them lets the hazard be fixed; only reportable events go to the HSA.'),
('What is the first thing to do on finding a colleague lying injured at the foot of a scaffold?',
 'Check it is safe to approach', ['Lift them to a sitting position', 'Fetch them a drink of water', 'Take a photo for the report'],
 'Make sure there is no further danger, such as falling objects or live cables, before going near. Then raise the alarm and get first aid; do not move a casualty with a possible spinal injury.'),
('Which phone numbers reach the emergency services in Ireland?',
 '112 or 999', ['911 or 112', '999 or 101', '000 or 112'],
 'Both 112 and 999 connect to the emergency services in Ireland, as An Garda Síochána advises. 911 is the North American number and 000 the Australian one.'),
('What information should a worker give first when calling 112 from a site?',
 "The site's exact location", ["The contractor's turnover", "The worker's PPS number", 'The time the shift began'],
 'The ambulance needs to find the site, so give the address, Eircode or directions first, then what happened and how many people are hurt.'),
('Who on site should a worker go to for first aid treatment?',
 'The trained occupational first aider', ['The nearest delivery driver on the site', 'The crane operator, who has a radio', 'Anyone on site who has a car'],
 'Employers must provide first aid equipment and trained occupational first aiders as the risk assessment requires. Workers should know who they are and where the kit is kept.'),
("Why should the injured person's own account be written down soon after an accident?",
 'Memories fade and details get lost', ['The HSA refuses any later reports', 'Insurers only pay claims made that day', 'The card holder must sign it on the day'],
 'Accurate early details help the investigation find the cause and prevent a repeat. Witness names, the time and what equipment was used should be noted.'),
('After a serious accident, why should the scene be left undisturbed where safe?',
 'So the cause can be investigated', ['So work can restart more quickly', 'So the PSDP can redesign the area', "So the injured worker's card can be renewed"],
 'Apart from making things safe and helping the injured, the scene should be left as it is so the HSA and the employer can find out what went wrong.'),
('What is the main purpose of investigating a site accident?',
 'To stop it happening again', ['To find someone to blame', 'To work out who pays fines', 'To decide who loses their card'],
 'Investigation looks for the root causes, including unsafe systems of work, so that controls can be improved for everyone on site.'),
('Where should a worker find out what to do and where to go if the fire alarm sounds on site?',
 'In the site induction and emergency plan', ['From the HSA website on the day', "From the client's building contract documents", 'From the back of the Safe Pass card'],
 'Each site has its own emergency arrangements, escape routes and assembly point, which are explained at the site-specific induction and displayed on site.'),
('A site siren sounds the evacuation signal while a worker is halfway through a cut. What should they do?',
 'Make the tool safe and go to the assembly point', ['Finish the cut, then leave', 'Collect their tools and van keys first', 'Wait in the canteen for further news'],
 'Stop work, make the area safe if it takes seconds, and go straight to the assembly point so the roll call can show who is missing.'),
('Which extinguisher does HSA guidance list for fires involving flammable liquids such as petrol or diesel?',
 'Foam', ['Water', 'Compressed air', 'A wet mop'],
 'The HSA fire extinguisher safety checks sheet lists foam for flammable liquid fires, carbon dioxide for electrical fires, and water, foam or multi-purpose powder for wood, paper and textiles. Water spreads burning liquid.'),
('Smoke is coming from a site distribution board. Which extinguisher should a trained worker avoid?',
 'Water', ['Carbon dioxide', 'Dry powder', 'None, as any type is fine'],
 'Water conducts electricity and can carry current back to the user. The HSA lists carbon dioxide for electrical fires; isolate the supply first if it is safe to do so.'),
('A small fire starts in a waste skip. When should a trained worker try to put it out?',
 'Only if it is safe and the alarm is raised', ['Always, before telling anyone else on site', 'Never, even if it is very small', 'Only once the Gardaí have arrived'],
 'Raise the alarm first. Tackle a small fire only if trained, with the right extinguisher and an escape route behind you; otherwise get out.'),
('Who should be told when a worker gets a minor cut that is treated on site?',
 'The supervisor, for the accident book', ['Nobody, as it is only minor', 'Only the HSA, within ten days', "Only the worker's own GP"],
 'Every injury, however minor, should be recorded on site. Minor ones are not reportable to the HSA but the record shows patterns and protects the worker.'),
]
